-      "text": "\nThe Construction Bot swings its hammer at you. You duck, and it takes out a mailbox instead.\n\n“Sorry, mailbox!”\n\nYou swing The Can Opener up, and the energy blade slices clean through the bot’s elbow. The hammer arm clangs to the pavement!\n\nThe bot spins around, confused, and the cabinet door on its belly flaps open. A screwdriver falls out. Then a tape measure. Then a very old sandwich. That’s when you see your opening: a panel on its back, right between the shoulders.\n\nOne more swing. The blade cuts the panel wide open, sparks fly, and the bot’s foot thrusters sputter out. It sits down with a big THUNK, like it’s taking a nap.\n\nIts eyes fade from angry red to a soft blue, then go dark.\n\nYou stand there breathing hard, holding a glowing wrench-axe in the middle of the street. “It worked,” you say. “It actually WORKED!”\n\nThen the blade flickers and shuts off on its own, and the handle’s hot.\n\n“Sir,” Asher says, “the power draw on that blade is enormous. I would not count on more than a minute at a time.”\n\nGood to know…would’ve been better to know a minute ago.\n",
+      "text": "\nThe Construction Bot swings its hammer at you. You duck, and it takes out a mailbox instead.\n\n“Sorry, mailbox! Am I talking to a mailbox right now? Focus up!”\n\nYou swing The Can Opener up, and the energy blade slices clean through the bot’s elbow. The hammer arm clangs to the pavement!\n\nThe bot spins around, confused, and the cabinet door on its belly flaps open from the sudden spin. Out falls a screwdriver, a tape measure, and then a very old sandwich, for some reason. That’s when you see it: a panel on its back, right between the shoulders.\n\nYou swing again. The blade cuts right through the panel and into a nest of wiring, sparks fly, and the bot’s foot thrusters sputter out. It falls over with a big THUNK.\n\n“It looks like it’s taking a nap, it’s kinda cute!” you say.\n\nIts eyes fade from angry red to a soft blue, then go dark.\n\nYou stand there sucking in air, holding a glowing wrench-axe in the middle of the street. “It worked,” you say. “It actually WORKED!”\n\nThen the blade flickers and shuts off on its own, and the handle’s hot.\n\n“Sir,” Asher says, “the power draw on that blade is enormous. I would not count on more than a minute at a time.”\n\nGood to know…I’ll have to keep that in mind.\n",
