       "title": "Neighborhood Safe",
-      "text": "\nYou take the long way home.\n\nThe streets of {world_name} are quiet, in a way they haven’t been for months.\n\nThere are robots everywhere, standing right where they stopped, with their eyes glowing a soft blue. People are coming out onto their porches in their pajamas to look at them, and nobody’s running. A little kid walks right up to a Construction Bot and pats it on its big round head. Down the block, a Long Range Security Bot is still giving everybody a thumbs-up, and for the first time, it kind of seems like it means it.\n\nMrs. Ruiz is standing outside her market with her broom. She sees a kid in goggles walk by under the streetlight, and she doesn’t say a word. She just taps two fingers to her forehead, like a salute.\n\nYou nod back, and you keep walking.\n\nYou pass the top of Main Street. Mr. Lin’s bench is still gone. There are four bolts in the sidewalk where it used to be, and nothing else.\n\nYou stand there for a minute. Nobody’s around. It’s just you and the streetlight and the quiet.\n\nSaturday. You’re going to play basketball with Mr. Lin on Saturday, and he’s going to beat you, and you’re going to argue about whether that last shot counted, and it’s going to be the best part of the whole week.\n\nYou’re going to build him a new bench first. A good one. Maybe with a cup holder.\n\nYou could use a normal day.\n\nYou keep walking, and the garage door rolls up, and there you stand, in front of your tools.\n\nHome.\n\nYou set The Can Opener down on the workbench. It’s dark, scorched, and completely drained, but it still has your dad’s initials stamped on the handle.\n\n“It did good,” you say.\n\n“So did you, sir,” says Asher.\n\nYou should sleep. You KNOW you should sleep. But your hands are already itching.\n",
+      "text": "\nYou take the long way home.\n\nThe streets of {world_name} are quiet, in a way they haven’t been for months.\n\nThere are robots everywhere, standing right where they stopped, with their eyes glowing a soft blue. People are coming out onto their porches in their pajamas to look at them, and nobody’s running. A little kid walks right up to a Construction Bot and pats it on its big round head. Down the block, a Long Range Security Bot is still giving everybody a thumbs-up, and for the first time, it kind of seems like it means it.\n\nMrs. Ruiz is standing outside her market with her broom. She sees a kid in goggles walk by under the streetlight, and she doesn’t say a word. She just taps two fingers to her forehead, like a salute.\n\nYou nod back, and you keep walking.\n\nYou pass the top of Main Street. Mr. Lin’s bench is still gone. There are four bolts in the sidewalk where it used to be, and nothing else.\n\nYou stand there for a minute. Nobody’s around. It’s just you and the streetlight and the quiet.\n\nSaturday. You’re going to play basketball with Mr. Lin on Saturday, and he’s going to beat you, and it’s going to be the best part of the whole week.\n\nYou’re going to build him a new bench first. A good one. Maybe with a cup holder.\n\nYou could use a normal day.\n\nYou keep walking, and the garage door rolls up, and there you stand, in front of your tools.\n\nHome.\n\nYou set The Can Opener down on the workbench. It’s dark, scorched, and completely drained, but it still has your dad’s initials stamped on the handle.\n\n“It did good,” you say.\n\n“So did you, sir,” says Asher.\n\nYou should sleep. You KNOW you should sleep. But your hands are already itching.\n",
